    "frames": [
      {
        "slide": 1,
        "role": "cover",
        "subject": "The kit pump jack seen side-on against a last-light horizon. Its walking beam, horsehead and A-frame Samson post are silhouetted, the crank arm catches the work light, and it stands on a skid on a dirt pad whose long cast shadows run toward the camera.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline a reader sees one working oil well at dusk, alone in the frame. A beam pump is itself a form of artificial lift, so the image still matches the new hook, and the dek's added sentence keeps the image from claiming the lift type the source never named.",
        "weakness": "Polish only. The scene is identical to the bound reading. The four-line dek clears the jack with sky between the type and the horsehead, and at 432 px the hook, dek and silhouette all read. Apart from the crank arm, the jack is close to pure black with thin blue rim slivers, so its paint and the chrome polished rod barely show.",
        "repair": "None required before the remaining frames. Optional polish, as bound: widen the work light so it rakes the walking beam's web and the horsehead face as well as the crank.",
        "encoding_reads": true,
        "dominant_subject_finished": true,
        "feed_legible": true,
        "inputs": {
